    "content": "IBANGA 8 — ISIZULU ULIMI LWASEKHAYA — TERM 2 — LESSON 2\nTOPIC: Ukufunda Nokubukela: Imibhalo Yolwazi Nezithombe\n\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES\nBy the end of this lesson, learners should be able to apply purpose, audience, main idea, supporting details, inference and visual clues; explain their choices; and complete the written and oral tasks with care.\n\nTEACHING NOTES\nInterview a community helper about a useful service. Prepare five open questions, listen without interrupting, record key points and present a one-minute summary. A good interview has an introduction, relevant questions, follow-up questions and a polite closing.\n\nKEY LEARNING\nPurpose, audience, main idea, supporting details, inference and visual clues are important parts of reading and viewing. Read the instructions carefully, identify the purpose of the task and use evidence or examples to support your answers. For Home Language learners, complete the main response in the target language and use the model below as a guide.\n\nMODEL / LANGUAGE EXAMPLE\nIsihlahla esidala sasipha abafundi umthunzi. Izinyoni zazicula emagatsheni aso.\n\nGUIDED PRACTICE\n1. Identify the purpose and audience of the model example.\n2. Underline or note two words that make the message clear and respectful.\n3. Explain how the example could be improved for a different audience.\n\nCLASSWORK\n1. Write the main idea of the lesson in one sentence.\n2. Identify three important details or features connected to the topic.\n3. Complete the task: Read the model text twice. Write two literal questions, two inferential questions and one question about the writer’s purpose. Support one answer with evidence.\n4. Share your response with a partner and give one helpful suggestion.\n\nHOMEWORK\nFind a suitable short text, poem or story. Record its title, main idea, two details and one new word with its meaning.\n\nKNOWLEDGE CHECK\n1. What is the main skill or language feature practised in this lesson?\n2. Why should a speaker or writer consider audience and purpose?\n3. Name two ways to improve the quality of your response.\n\nANSWERS / MARKING GUIDE\n1. Accept a relevant explanation of purpose, audience, main idea, supporting details, inference and visual clues.\n2. Audience and purpose help a speaker or writer choose suitable content, vocabulary, tone and format.\n3. Any two: plan, listen carefully, use evidence, organise ideas, check grammar, edit spelling and punctuation, or respond respectfully.\nCLASSWORK: Accept relevant, well-supported answers. For oral tasks assess clarity, listening, relevance and respectful turn-taking. For written tasks assess content (4), organisation (2), language and vocabulary (2), spelling and punctuation (2). Total 10 marks.\n\nTERM 2 CONSOLIDATION\nWrite a friendly letter or email to a friend explaining a positive change at your school. Include a greeting, opening, three organised points, closing and your name. Plan, draft, edit and produce a final copy.\n\nTEACHER NOTE\nThis is a structured CAPS-aligned learning resource organised by language skill strands. Teachers should check the school’s approved Annual Teaching Plan, prescribed literature and provincial language conventions, and adapt examples to the learners’ context before formal assessment. Home Language examples should be checked by a fluent subject educator."
