    "title": "Ukubhala Nokwethula: Izincwadi Ezisemthethweni Nemibiko",
    "content": "IBANGA 8 — ISIZULU ULIMI LWASEKHAYA — TERM 4 — LESSON 3\nTOPIC: Ukubhala Nokwethula: Izincwadi Ezisemthethweni Nemibiko\n\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES\nBy the end of this lesson, learners should be able to apply formal register, layout, purpose, factual accuracy and coherent paragraphs; explain their choices; and complete the written and oral tasks with care.\n\nTEACHING NOTES\nListen to a set of spoken instructions and write them in the correct sequence. Identify key details, ask for clarification and give a concise oral summary. Practise note-taking using short phrases rather than copying every word.\n\nKEY LEARNING\nFormal register, layout, purpose, factual accuracy and coherent paragraphs are important parts of writing and presenting. Read the instructions carefully, identify the purpose of the task and use evidence or examples to support your answers. For Home Language learners, complete the main response in the target language and use the model below as a guide.\n\nMODEL / LANGUAGE EXAMPLE\nMngani wami, ngiyakubhalela ngikuxoxele ngosuku lokuhlanza isikole. Sasebenza ndawonye.\n\nGUIDED PRACTICE\n1. Identify the purpose and audience of the model example.\n2. Underline or note two words that make the message clear and respectful.\n3. Explain how the example could be improved for a different audience.\n\nCLASSWORK\n1. Write the main idea of the lesson in one sentence.\n2. Identify three important details or features connected to the topic.\n3. Complete the task: Plan and write a text of 100–120 words for the stated audience. Use a suitable structure, linking words and a clear ending.\n4. Share your response with a partner and give one helpful suggestion.\n\nHOMEWORK\nEdit your classwork, produce a neat final copy and check spelling, punctuation, paragraphing and audience.\n\nKNOWLEDGE CHECK\n1. What is the main skill or language feature practised in this lesson?\n2. Why should a speaker or writer consider audience and purpose?\n3. Name two ways to improve the quality of your response.\n\nANSWERS / MARKING GUIDE\n1. Accept a relevant explanation of formal register, layout, purpose, factual accuracy and coherent paragraphs.\n2. Audience and purpose help a speaker or writer choose suitable content, vocabulary, tone and format.\n3. Any two: plan, listen carefully, use evidence, organise ideas, check grammar, edit spelling and punctuation, or respond respectfully.\nCLASSWORK: Accept relevant, well-supported answers. For oral tasks assess clarity, listening, relevance and respectful turn-taking. For written tasks assess content (4), organisation (2), language and vocabulary (2), spelling and punctuation (2). Total 10 marks.\n\nTERM 4 CONSOLIDATION\nWrite a formal letter to the school principal suggesting one improvement. Include the recipient, date, suitable greeting, purpose, supporting reasons, respectful request and formal closing. Also practise a short factual report with a heading and ordered details.\n\nTEACHER NOTE\nThis is a structured CAPS-aligned learning resource organised by language skill strands. Teachers should check the school’s approved Annual Teaching Plan, prescribed literature and provincial language conventions, and adapt examples to the learners’ context before formal assessment. Home Language examples should be checked by a fluent subject educator."
