    "subject": "itsonga HL",
    "term": 3,
    "title": "Ku Yingisela na Ku Vulavula: Mbulavurisano na Mavonelo",
    "content": "Giredi ya 8 — Xitsonga Ririmi ra le Kaya — TERM 3 — LESSON 1\nTOPIC: Ku Yingisela na Ku Vulavula: Mbulavurisano na Mavonelo\n\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES\nBy the end of this lesson, learners should be able to apply turn-taking, expressing opinions, giving reasons, respectful disagreement and group reflection; explain their choices; and complete the written and oral tasks with care.\n\nTEACHING NOTES\nTake part in a small-group discussion about whether learners should take part in community service. State a clear view, give a reason and example, invite another speaker and respond respectfully. Summarise the group's conclusion.\n\nKEY LEARNING\nTurn-taking, expressing opinions, giving reasons, respectful disagreement and group reflection are important parts of listening and speaking. Read the instructions carefully, identify the purpose of the task and use evidence or examples to support your answers. For Home Language learners, complete the main response in the target language and use the model below as a guide.\n\nMODEL / LANGUAGE EXAMPLE\n“Avuxeni. Ha khensa ku bula na hina. U pfuna vaaki hi ndlela yihi?”\n\nGUIDED PRACTICE\n1. Identify the purpose and audience of the model example.\n2. Underline or note two words that make the message clear and respectful.\n3. Explain how the example could be improved for a different audience.\n\nCLASSWORK\n1. Write the main idea of the lesson in one sentence.\n2. Identify three important details or features connected to the topic.\n3. Complete the task: Prepare five questions and conduct a short paired interview. Take notes and give a one-minute oral summary.\n4. Share your response with a partner and give one helpful suggestion.\n\nHOMEWORK\nInterview a family or community member. Write a short summary of the information and bring three useful facts to class.\n\nKNOWLEDGE CHECK\n1. What is the main skill or language feature practised in this lesson?\n2. Why should a speaker or writer consider audience and purpose?\n3. Name two ways to improve the quality of your response.\n\nANSWERS / MARKING GUIDE\n1. Accept a relevant explanation of turn-taking, expressing opinions, giving reasons, respectful disagreement and group reflection.\n2. Audience and purpose help a speaker or writer choose suitable content, vocabulary, tone and format.\n3. Any two: plan, listen carefully, use evidence, organise ideas, check grammar, edit spelling and punctuation, or respond respectfully.\nCLASSWORK: Accept relevant, well-supported answers. For oral tasks assess clarity, listening, relevance and respectful turn-taking. For written tasks assess content (4), organisation (2), language and vocabulary (2), spelling and punctuation (2). Total 10 marks.\n\nTERM 3 CONSOLIDATION\nWrite a descriptive piece about a place that is important to your community. Use sensory details, precise vocabulary, a clear order and a concluding sentence. Then write a short narrative paragraph showing a character responding to a challenge.\n\nTEACHER NOTE\nThis is a structured CAPS-aligned learning resource organised by language skill strands. Teachers should check the school’s approved Annual Teaching Plan, prescribed literature and provincial language conventions, and adapt examples to the learners’ context before formal assessment. Home Language examples should be checked by a fluent subject educator."
